.\" test/unit/test_manual.ml checks the options of typec listed under
.\" DESCRIPTION and the statuses listed under EXIT STATUS against the
.\" driver's tables.
.\"
.Dd November 17, 2026
.Dt TYPEC 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm typec
.Nd Type-C compiler
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -cc Ns = Ns Ar path
.Op Fl -stats
.Op Fl -trap-function Ns = Ns Ar name
.Op Ar cc-option ...
.Ar file ...
.Nm
.Op Fl -cc Ns = Ns Ar path
.Op Fl -stats
.Op Fl -trap-function Ns = Ns Ar name
.Fl -emit-c
.Op Fl o Ar output
.Op Ar cc-option ...
.Ar file.tc
.Nm
.Op Fl -cc Ns = Ns Ar path
.Op Fl -stats
.Op Fl -trap-function Ns = Ns Ar name
.Fl -emit-c-header
.Op Fl o Ar output
.Op Ar cc-option ...
.Ar file.tci
.Nm
.Op Fl -cc Ns = Ns Ar path
.Fl -print-target
.Op Ar cc-option ...
.Nm
.Op Fl -cc Ns = Ns Ar path
.Fl -version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Type-C is C11 with variants, generics and errors as values.
.Nm
compiles it to C and hands that C to a C compiler, the
.Em backend ,
which compiles and links it as it would any C.
.Nm
takes the command line of
.Xr cc 1
and passes every input that is not a Type-C file, with its options, to
the backend untouched, so it can stand in for
.Xr cc 1
in a build.
.Pp
An input whose name ends in
.Pa .tc ,
with no
.Fl x
other than
.Fl x Cm none
before it, is a Type-C unit.
.Nm
runs the backend's preprocessor on it, reads, checks and lowers the
preprocessed text to C, and has the backend compile that C as it would
compile the unit's
.Pa .c
file: the output, the dependency files of
.Fl MD
and
.Fl MMD ,
and the rule of
.Fl M
are named as for a
.Pa .c
input.
.Nm
stops at the first error in the source and prints it as
.Ar file : Ns Ar line : Li error : Ar message .
Without a
.Pa .tc
input
.Nm
replaces itself with the backend.
.Pp
The options of
.Nm
itself never reach the backend; each is one wherever an option can
stand, but not as the value of another option such as
.Fl o .
They are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -cc Ns = Ns Ar path
Use the C compiler at
.Ar path
as the backend.
The last one given wins.
.It Fl -emit-c
Stop after lowering and write the C of the one
.Pa .tc
input to the file that
.Fl o
names, or to standard output.
.It Fl -emit-c-header
Write the C header of the one
.Pa .tci
input, so that C code can use its types, and compile nothing.
.Ar M Ns Pa .tci
gives
.Ar M Ns Pa .h
beside it, or the file that
.Fl o
names, or standard output under
.Fl o Cm - .
The dependency options work as for a
.Pa .tc
input, with the header as the target of the rule, which names the
interface and every file that it includes.
.Fl MD
and
.Fl MMD
write the rule to the header's path followed by
.Pa .d ,
unless
.Fl MF
names another file.
.Fl M
and
.Fl MM
print the rule, or write it to the file that
.Fl MF
names, and write no header, so they cannot be used with
.Fl o .
Under
.Fl o Cm - ,
.Fl MD
and
.Fl MMD
need
.Fl MT
or
.Fl MQ ,
and
.Fl MF .
.It Fl -print-target
Run the backend with the other arguments as options, which select the
target, and print the target model that its predefined macros give, one
.Ar field : Ar value
per line.
.It Fl -stats
Print on standard error what each
.Pa .tc
input, or the
.Pa .tci
input of
.Fl -emit-c-header ,
counts against the translation limits of Type-C.
.It Fl -trap-function Ns = Ns Ar name
Make every trap of the emitted C, such as a slice index out of range,
call the function
.Ft _Noreturn void
.Fn name void ,
which the program supplies, before
.Fn __builtin_trap .
.Ar name
is a C identifier of ASCII letters, digits and underscores that does
not start with a digit; the last
one given wins.
.It Fl -version
Run the backend with
.Fl -version
alone, pass on its output, then print
.Nm
and its release on a last line, so that a build tool that reads the
output sees the backend.
Nothing is compiled, whatever the rest of the command line asks, but an
error in one of the options of
.Nm
is still one.
The status is the backend's, or 81 when the backend cannot be started,
and a backend that fails or cannot be started is named on standard
error.
.El
.Ss Choosing the backend
The backend is the program that the last
.Fl -cc Ns = Ns Ar path
names, else the one that
.Ev TYPEC_CC
names when it is set and not empty, else
.Xr cc 1 .
A name without a slash is looked up in
.Ev PATH .
.Nm
runs the backend with an argument vector, never through a shell, so
.Ev TYPEC_CC
names one program and cannot carry arguments of its own.
The backend is GCC or Clang, Apple's included, of the releases that the
README names;
.Nm
refuses an older one.
.Ss Options refused with a Type-C input
With a
.Pa .tc
input, or under
.Fl -emit-c-header ,
.Nm
refuses, as an error of the command line, the options that it cannot
follow:
.Fl E ;
the options that change the preprocessed text, such as
.Fl P ,
.Fl C
and every
.Fl d
option of dumps;
.Fl save-temps ,
.Fl ### ,
.Fl ansi
and
.Fl - ;
response files and the options that name a file of more options, such
as
.Fl specs= ;
the queries that print a fact of the backend, such as
.Fl print-file-name= ;
the options that change a type of C that the target model cannot see,
such as
.Fl funsigned-bitfields ;
.Fl arch
naming more than one architecture; a
.Fl std=
that names no form of C11 or C17; and every long option, spelled with
.Ql -- ,
that it does not know.
The README lists them with the reason for each.
Without a
.Pa .tc
input the backend sees them all.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "TYPEC_OUTER_CC"
.It Ev TYPEC_CC
The backend, when no
.Fl -cc Ns = Ns Ar path
is given.
.It Ev TYPEC_OUTER_CC
.Nm
sets it to the name of the backend in the environment of every backend
that it runs.
A
.Nm
started with it set exits with an error, as the backend would otherwise
run
.Nm
again forever; to install
.Nm
as
.Xr cc 1 ,
set
.Ev TYPEC_CC
to the real C compiler.
.It Ev TMPDIR
The directory in which
.Nm
makes its private temporary directory, which it removes on every way
out; by default
.Pa /tmp .
.It Ev PATH
Where a backend named without a slash is looked up.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "M.tci"
.It Pa *.tc
A Type-C unit, compiled as a
.Pa .c
file is.
.It Pa *.tci
A Type-C interface: a header that Type-C units include.
.It Ar M Ns Pa .h
The C header that
.Fl -emit-c-header
writes for
.Ar M Ns Pa .tci .
It begins with a line that says it is generated, and
.Nm
overwrites no other file.
.It Ar M Ns Pa .h.d
The dependency rule of
.Ar M Ns Pa .h
under
.Fl MD
or
.Fl MMD
with
.Fl -emit-c-header .
.Nm
replaces only a regular file there.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
When
.Nm
replaces itself with the backend, the status is the backend's.
Otherwise
.Nm
exits 0 when every step succeeds, with the status of the first backend
step that fails, or ends by the signal that ended it;
.Fl -print-target
exits 81 when the backend fails.
Its own failures have statuses chosen apart from those of C compiler
drivers:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 80
The program is refused: an error in the source.
.It 81
An error of the environment: the command line, a backend that cannot be
started or gives no target model, or a file that cannot be read or
written.
.It 82
An internal error, a bug in
.Nm .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Compile a Type-C unit to an object:
.Pp
.Dl $ typec -c area.tc
.Pp
Build a program from a Type-C unit and a C file, with GCC as the
backend:
.Pp
.Dl $ typec --cc=gcc -o shape shape.tc util.c
.Pp
Print the C that
.Nm
hands to the backend:
.Pp
.Dl $ typec --emit-c token.tc
.Pp
Write
.Pa port.h
for the C code that uses the types of
.Pa port.tci :
.Pp
.Dl $ typec --emit-c-header port.tci
.Pp
Build a project with
.Nm
in place of
.Xr cc 1 :
.Pp
.Dl $ make CC=typec
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cc 1 ,
.Xr clang 1 ,
.Xr gcc 1
.Pp
The README and the language design of the source distribution:
.Lk https://github.com/stass/type-c
